1
00:00:03,839 --> 00:00:08,440
Chapter 1. My father’s family name being Pirrip, and my Christian name Philip, my infant

2
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:13,359
tongue could make of both names nothing longer or more explicit than Pip. So,

3
00:00:13,359 --> 00:00:17,440
I called myself Pip, and came to be called Pip.

4
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:22,359
I give Pirrip as my father’s family name, on the authority of his tombstone and my sister,—Mrs.

5
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:29,759
Joe Gargery, who married the blacksmith. As I never saw my father or my mother, and never

6
00:00:29,759 --> 00:00:34,960
saw any likeness of either of them (for their days were long before the days of photographs),

7
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:40,920
my first fancies regarding what they were like were unreasonably derived from their tombstones.

8
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:46,640
The shape of the letters on my father’s, gave me an odd idea that he was a square, stout, dark man,

9
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:52,799
with curly black hair. From the character and turn of the inscription, “Also Georgiana Wife

10
00:00:52,799 --> 00:00:59,200
of the Above,” I drew a childish conclusion that my mother was freckled and sickly. To five little

11
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:04,480
stone lozenges, each about a foot and a half long, which were arranged in a neat row beside

12
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:10,239
their grave, and were sacred to the memory of five little brothers of mine,—who gave up trying to

13
00:01:10,239 --> 00:01:16,840
get a living, exceedingly early in that universal struggle,—I am indebted for a belief I religiously

14
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:21,920
entertained that they had all been born on their backs with their hands in their trousers-pockets,

15
00:01:22,799 --> 00:01:26,879
and had never taken them out in this state of existence.

16
00:01:26,879 --> 00:01:34,040
Ours was the marsh country, down by the river, within, as the river wound, twenty miles of the

17
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:39,719
sea. My first most vivid and broad impression of the identity of things seems to me to have

18
00:01:39,719 --> 00:01:46,280
been gained on a memorable raw afternoon towards evening. At such a time I found out for certain

19
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:52,159
that this bleak place overgrown with nettles was the churchyard; and that Philip Pirrip, late

20
00:01:52,159 --> 00:01:58,400
of this parish, and also Georgiana wife of the above, were dead and buried; and that Alexander,

21
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:05,120
Bartholomew, Abraham, Tobias, and Roger, infant children of the aforesaid, were also dead and

22
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:11,800
buried; and that the dark flat wilderness beyond the churchyard, intersected with dikes and mounds

23
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:18,599
and gates, with scattered cattle feeding on it, was the marshes; and that the low leaden line

24
00:02:18,599 --> 00:02:25,879
beyond was the river; and that the distant savage lair from which the wind was rushing was the sea;

25
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:33,520
and that the small bundle of shivers growing afraid of it all and beginning to cry, was Pip.

26
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:39,039
“Hold your noise!” cried a terrible voice, as a man started up from among the graves

27
00:02:39,039 --> 00:02:45,080
at the side of the church porch. “Keep still, you little devil, or I’ll cut your throat!”

28
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:51,879
A fearful man, all in coarse grey, with a great iron on his leg. A man with no hat,

29
00:02:51,879 --> 00:02:57,439
and with broken shoes, and with an old rag tied round his head. A man who had been

30
00:02:57,439 --> 00:03:03,360
soaked in water, and smothered in mud, and lamed by stones, and cut by flints,

31
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:09,439
and stung by nettles, and torn by briars; who limped, and shivered, and glared,

32
00:03:09,439 --> 00:03:14,840
and growled; and whose teeth chattered in his head as he seized me by the chin.

33
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:19,840
“Oh! Don’t cut my throat, sir,” I pleaded in terror. “Pray don’t do it, sir.”

34
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:23,479
“Tell us your name!” said the man. “Quick!”

35
00:03:23,479 --> 00:03:25,159
“Pip, sir.”

36
00:03:25,159 --> 00:03:29,680
“Once more,” said the man, staring at me. “Give it mouth!”

37
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:31,520
“Pip. Pip, sir.”

38
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:35,879
“Show us where you live,” said the man. “Pint out the place!”

39
00:03:35,879 --> 00:03:37,560
I pointed to where our village lay,

40
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:43,439
on the flat in-shore among the alder-trees and pollards, a mile or more from the church.

41
00:03:43,439 --> 00:03:47,520
The man, after looking at me for a moment, turned me upside down,

42
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:54,240
and emptied my pockets. There was nothing in them but a piece of bread. When the church

43
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:59,800
came to itself,—for he was so sudden and strong that he made it go head over heels before me,

44
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:03,840
and I saw the steeple under my feet,—when the church came to itself,

45
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:10,839
I say, I was seated on a high tombstone, trembling while he ate the bread ravenously.

46
00:04:10,839 --> 00:04:17,199
“You young dog,” said the man, licking his lips, “what fat cheeks you ha’ got.”

47
00:04:17,199 --> 00:04:24,160
I believe they were fat, though I was at that time undersized for my years, and not strong.

48
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:26,800
“Darn me if I couldn’t eat ’em,” said the man,

49
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:32,120
with a threatening shake of his head, “and if I han’t half a mind to’t!”

50
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:36,519
I earnestly expressed my hope that he wouldn’t, and held tighter to the tombstone on which he

51
00:04:36,519 --> 00:04:42,800
had put me; partly, to keep myself upon it; partly, to keep myself from crying.

52
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:46,519
“Now lookee here!” said the man. “Where’s your mother?”

53
00:04:46,519 --> 00:04:48,120
“There, sir!” said I.

54
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:51,639
He started, made a short run, and stopped and looked over his shoulder.

55
00:04:52,399 --> 00:04:58,160
“There, sir!” I timidly explained. “Also Georgiana. That’s my mother.”

56
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:03,639
“Oh!” said he, coming back. “And is that your father alonger your mother?”

57
00:05:03,639 --> 00:05:08,279
“Yes, sir,” said I; “him too; late of this parish.”

58
00:05:08,279 --> 00:05:14,600
“Ha!” he muttered then, considering. “Who d’ye live with,—supposin’ you’re kindly let to live,

59
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:17,399
which I han’t made up my mind about?”

60
00:05:17,399 --> 00:05:22,879
“My sister, sir,—Mrs. Joe Gargery,—wife of Joe Gargery, the blacksmith, sir.”

61
00:05:22,879 --> 00:05:27,680
“Blacksmith, eh?” said he. And looked down at his leg.

62
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:33,040
After darkly looking at his leg and me several times, he came closer to my tombstone,

63
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:37,839
took me by both arms, and tilted me back as far as he could hold me;

64
00:05:37,839 --> 00:05:41,399
so that his eyes looked most powerfully down into mine,

65
00:05:41,399 --> 00:05:47,439
and mine looked most helplessly up into his. “Now lookee here,” he said, “the question

66
00:05:47,439 --> 00:05:51,720
being whether you’re to be let to live. You know what a file is?”

67
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:53,040
“Yes, sir.”

68
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:55,160
“And you know what wittles is?”

69
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:56,920
“Yes, sir.”

70
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:59,160
After each question he tilted me over a little

71
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:03,399
more, so as to give me a greater sense of helplessness and danger.

72
00:06:03,399 --> 00:06:12,759
“You get me a file.” He tilted me again. “And you get me wittles.” He tilted me again. “You bring

73
00:06:12,759 --> 00:06:19,920
’em both to me.” He tilted me again. “Or I’ll have your heart and liver out.” He tilted me again.

74
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:24,040
I was dreadfully frightened, and so giddy that I clung to him with both hands,

75
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:27,480
and said, “If you would kindly please to let me keep upright,

76
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:33,120
sir, perhaps I shouldn’t be sick, and perhaps I could attend more.”

77
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:35,839
He gave me a most tremendous dip and roll,

78
00:06:35,839 --> 00:06:41,120
so that the church jumped over its own weathercock. Then, he held me by the arms,

79
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:46,639
in an upright position on the top of the stone, and went on in these fearful terms:—

80
00:06:46,639 --> 00:06:52,399
“You bring me, to-morrow morning early, that file and them wittles. You bring the lot to me,

81
00:06:52,399 --> 00:06:58,079
at that old Battery over yonder. You do it, and you never dare to say a word or dare

82
00:06:58,079 --> 00:07:04,639
to make a sign concerning your having seen such a person as me, or any person sumever,

83
00:07:04,639 --> 00:07:10,959
and you shall be let to live. You fail, or you go from my words in any partickler,

84
00:07:10,959 --> 00:07:18,240
no matter how small it is, and your heart and your liver shall be tore out, roasted, and ate. Now,

85
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:24,040
I ain’t alone, as you may think I am. There’s a young man hid with me, in comparison with which

86
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:30,720
young man I am a Angel. That young man hears the words I speak. That young man has a secret

87
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:39,680
way pecooliar to himself, of getting at a boy, and at his heart, and at his liver. It is in wain for

88
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:47,639
a boy to attempt to hide himself from that young man. A boy may lock his door, may be warm in bed,

89
00:07:47,639 --> 00:07:55,040
may tuck himself up, may draw the clothes over his head, may think himself comfortable and safe,

90
00:07:55,040 --> 00:08:00,160
but that young man will softly creep and creep his way to him and tear him open. I

91
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:07,759
am a keeping that young man from harming of you at the present moment, with great difficulty.

92
00:08:07,759 --> 00:08:13,759
I find it wery hard to hold that young man off of your inside. Now, what do you say?”

93
00:08:13,759 --> 00:08:19,319
I said that I would get him the file, and I would get him what broken bits of food I could,

94
00:08:19,319 --> 00:08:23,360
and I would come to him at the Battery, early in the morning.

95
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:27,600
“Say Lord strike you dead if you don’t!” said the man.

96
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,240
I said so, and he took me down.

97
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:34,279
“Now,” he pursued, “you remember what you’ve undertook, and you

98
00:08:34,279 --> 00:08:37,720
remember that young man, and you get home!”

99
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:41,120
“Goo-good night, sir,” I faltered.

100
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:42,919
“Much of that!” said he,

101
00:08:42,919 --> 00:08:49,080
glancing about him over the cold wet flat. “I wish I was a frog. Or a eel!”

102
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:54,080
At the same time, he hugged his shuddering body in both his arms,—clasping himself,

103
00:08:54,080 --> 00:09:01,000
as if to hold himself together,—and limped towards the low church wall. As I saw him go,

104
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,279
picking his way among the nettles, and among the brambles that bound the green mounds,

105
00:09:06,279 --> 00:09:10,440
he looked in my young eyes as if he were eluding the hands of the dead people,

106
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:16,639
stretching up cautiously out of their graves, to get a twist upon his ankle and pull him in.

107
00:09:16,639 --> 00:09:23,039
When he came to the low church wall, he got over it, like a man whose legs were numbed and stiff,

108
00:09:23,039 --> 00:09:29,679
and then turned round to look for me. When I saw him turning, I set my face towards home,

109
00:09:29,679 --> 00:09:35,080
and made the best use of my legs. But presently I looked over my shoulder,

110
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:40,279
and saw him going on again towards the river, still hugging himself in both arms,

111
00:09:40,279 --> 00:09:43,799
and picking his way with his sore feet among the great stones dropped into the

112
00:09:43,799 --> 00:09:50,559
marshes here and there, for stepping-places when the rains were heavy or the tide was in.

113
00:09:50,559 --> 00:09:56,240
The marshes were just a long black horizontal line then, as I stopped to look after him;

114
00:09:56,240 --> 00:10:01,919
and the river was just another horizontal line, not nearly so broad nor yet so black;

115
00:10:01,919 --> 00:10:09,279
and the sky was just a row of long angry red lines and dense black lines intermixed. On the

116
00:10:09,279 --> 00:10:13,840
edge of the river I could faintly make out the only two black things in all the prospect that

117
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:19,919
seemed to be standing upright; one of these was the beacon by which the sailors steered,—like

118
00:10:19,919 --> 00:10:27,480
an unhooped cask upon a pole,—an ugly thing when you were near it; the other, a gibbet,

119
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:33,559
with some chains hanging to it which had once held a pirate. The man was limping on towards

120
00:10:33,559 --> 00:10:39,679
this latter, as if he were the pirate come to life, and come down, and going back to hook

121
00:10:39,679 --> 00:10:45,679
himself up again. It gave me a terrible turn when I thought so; and as I saw the cattle

122
00:10:45,679 --> 00:10:52,159
lifting their heads to gaze after him, I wondered whether they thought so too. I looked all round

123
00:10:52,159 --> 00:10:57,320
for the horrible young man, and could see no signs of him. But now I was frightened again,

124
00:10:57,320 --> 00:11:00,984
and ran home without stopping. Chapter 2.
